    <h1>State-Regulated Life Insurance: The Government Benefit Hoax</h1>
    <ArticleByline />
    <div class="inv__summary">
      <ArticleSummary quickAnswer={quickAnswer} takeaways={{ label: 'Key Points', items: keyPoints }} />
    </div>

    <div class="inv__intro">
      <p>I've sold life insurance for over a decade, and one of the most deceptive marketing tactics I've seen is insurance advertising disguised as official government notices. These mailers use phrases like "state-regulated benefits" to create a false sense of authority, leaving seniors confused, misled, and wondering whether they've been scammed. Read this entire article to see how these mailers mislead consumers, then use my <a href="/free-quote/">free life insurance quoting tool</a> right here on this page to compare actual rates from multiple insurance companies.</p>
    </div>

    <section class="inv__mailer" aria-labelledby="mailer-2026">
      <div class="inv__banner"><h2 id="mailer-2026">8 Misleading Claims on This Life Insurance Mailer</h2></div>
      <p>Here's an example of a 2026 state-regulated life insurance mailer. I've numbered eight claims on it that I think every senior ought to understand before mailing anything back.</p>
      <MailerDecoder part="figure" />
      <MailerDecoder part="claims" heading={false} />
    </section>

    <section class="inv__mailer" aria-labelledby="mailer-2027">
      <div class="inv__banner"><h2 id="mailer-2027">Another Year. Another State-Regulated Life Insurance Mailer.</h2></div>
      <p>Here's another example, this time advertising up to $35,000 for 2027. The wording has changed, but take a look at how many of the same marketing tactics show up again.</p>
      <MailerDecoder part="figure" mailer="2027" />
      <MailerDecoder part="claims" mailer="2027" />
    </section>

    <section class="inv__mailer" aria-labelledby="mailer-notice">
