      <MailerDecoder part="figure" mailer="notice" />
      <MailerDecoder part="claims" mailer="notice" />
    </section>

    <h2 id="regulators">What Insurance Regulators Discovered About These Mailers</h2>
    <p>I don't know who sent this particular card, and I'm not going to guess. But state insurance regulators have looked at mailers built exactly like it, and what they found is worth knowing.</p>
    <div class="inv__findings">
      <section class="inv__finding">
        <h3>Washington, D.C. (2020): A Consumer Warning</h3>
        <p>The D.C. Department of Insurance, Securities and Banking (DISB) warned residents about a mailer titled "F-1 2020 Benefit Information for District of Columbia Residents Only." DISB said the mailer didn't come from the District government and wasn't sanctioned by DISB, and that the information people sent back could be used by, or sold to, insurance companies so their agents could contact them.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="inv__finding">
        <h3>Iowa (2020): A Cease-and-Desist Order</h3>
        <p>Two Georgia companies, Direct Mail Processing, LLC and Rehg Data Assets, Inc., sent official-looking insurance mailers to older Iowans. One of them referred to a "state-regulated program" for final expenses. People who returned a card got a call from an insurance salesperson, and the mailer company was paid for each lead. The Iowa Insurance Division ordered the operation to cease and desist. Separately, the two companies reached a consent judgment with the Iowa Attorney General's Office: they agreed to pay the state $40,000 and denied violating Iowa's Consumer Fraud Act.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="inv__finding">
        <h3>Nebraska (2022): Consumer Education</h3>
        <p>In a presentation on misleading insurance marketing, the Nebraska Department of Insurance showed a "T-2" card headed "2020 Benefit Information for Nebraska Citizens Only" and explained how lead generation works.</p>
      </section>
    </div>
    <p>The Texas Department of Insurance sums it up in its consumer fraud guide: "The government doesn't sell insurance. Agents who say they're with the government are breaking the law."</p>
    <p>Different states, different letters in the corner of the card, same basic idea. Those findings are about those mailers; they don't identify who sent the 2026 card above or show that its sender broke the law.</p>

    <h2 id="before-you-respond">Before You Respond to This Mailer, Do This Instead</h2>
    <p>Your state doesn't sponsor, fund or give away private life insurance to its residents. The real government options are limited and specific: Social Security's one-time $255 death payment to an eligible spouse or child, VA burial benefits and VALife for eligible veterans, and a few others for particular groups.</p>
    <ol class="inv__cards">
      <li class="inv__card-cta"><strong>You Don't Have to Answer the Card</strong> <span>You can skip the mailer entirely. Call Funeral Funds and we'll help you cut through the confusion, compare your options, and find coverage that fits your needs and budget.</span> <span>Or get started online with a free quote.</span> <a class="btn art-cta__btn" href="https://funeralfunds.com/free-quote/">Get Your Free Quote</a></li>
      <li>If an agent calls, ask which insurance company the policy is with, what type of coverage it is, and whether it has a waiting period. Ask what your family would receive if you died in the first two years.</li>
      <li>Check that the agent and the company are licensed through your state's insurance department.</li>
      <li>Answer the health questions before you settle for a waiting period. Your actual price and eligibility depend on your age, health, coverage amount and the insurance company.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The vast majority of the people I help qualify for first-day coverage. My process is simple. I ask a few short health questions, compare rates from around 20 to 30 insurance companies, depending on your state, age, and health, and show you the first-day coverage options available to you. If you'd like me to look at an offer you received, call me at <a href={lead.phone.href}>{lead.phone.display}</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="send-back">What Happens When You Send the Card Back?</h2>
    <p>Here's how a reply card can turn into a phone call:</p>
    <ol class="inv__cards">
      <li><strong>You fill it out.</strong> Name, home address, phone number, age, maybe your spouse's name and age. Then you drop it in the mail.</li>
      <li><strong>Your reply becomes a lead.</strong> Some companies specialize in mailing these cards and selling the replies to insurance agents.</li>
      <li><strong>An agent buys it.</strong> In my experience, an agent may pay around $40, $50 or $60 for each lead, or some other amount, depending on the lead program.</li>
      <li><strong>The agent contacts you.</strong> That's the phone call, or the knock on the door, that the card was really set up to produce.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The agent who buys your lead may represent one insurance company or several. What you're offered depends on which products that agent has, how well the agent knows underwriting (which companies accept which health conditions), and your own health and budget.</p>
    <p>Some agents may lean toward guaranteed-acceptance insurance because it doesn't ask health questions and can be easier to place. That isn't true of every agent, and you can't tell how an agent works, or how they're paid, just from the mailer. Guaranteed acceptance can be the right fit for someone who can't qualify for simplified issue coverage. But it generally costs more than first-day coverage for a comparable applicant, and it has a waiting period before the full benefit is paid for a death from natural causes.</p>

    <h2 id="right-plan">Finding the Right Life Insurance Plan</h2>
    <p>Finding the right life insurance plan isn't about responding to the most official-looking advertisement or choosing the company with the biggest advertised benefit. It's about finding coverage that actually fits your needs, your health, and your budget.</p>
    <p>I've spent 11 years helping people compare life insurance companies, and one of the biggest mistakes I see is people buying a policy without knowing what else is available.</p>
    <p>Some people can qualify for coverage that pays from the first day. Others may need a policy with a waiting period. The important thing is knowing which options are actually available to you before making a decision.</p>
    <p>And sometimes, if you already have enough money set aside to cover your final expenses, you may not need another insurance policy at all.</p>
    <p>My job isn't to sell you whatever happens to be advertised in your mailbox. It's to help you find out whether you need coverage and, if you do, which available policy makes the most sense.</p>

